          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>, who initiated it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao, the reformers who led it; Tan Sitong, a reformist thinker; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>, who ended it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> and Ronglu, whose forces supported the coup</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">most measures faced resistance, and the coup of September 1898 put the emperor under house arrest and ended the programme; the Imperial University of Peking, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>, survived, and later Qing reforms followed after 1901</span></div>
