    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The reform was a response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s defeat by Meiji <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> (1894–1895), which Chinese elites took as evidence that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>'s focus on weapons and machinery had failed.</li><li>Over roughly 100 days the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> issued edicts on education, administration, the economy and the military, among them the founding of the Imperial University of Peking, but most measures met resistance from conservatives.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> ended the programme with a coup in September 1898, placed the emperor under house arrest and had the leading reformers ordered executed; she later backed the late Qing reforms.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The Imperial University of Peking, set up during the Hundred Days' Reform, is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>. Guangxu, put under house arrest on Yingtai Island after the coup, remained there until his death in 1908.</p></aside>
    </section>

