    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">1839</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-opium-war">First Opium War</a> begins, the first of two defeats that started <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s effort to modernise</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1865</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a> is established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and becomes the largest factory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1894</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> begins; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> is defeated by Meiji <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> cedes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> Peninsula</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> begins to issue the reform edicts, on 11 June</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1898</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> stages a coup, places the emperor under house arrest and orders the leading reformers executed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1905</span><span class="rf-text">The imperial examination is abolished, one of the late Qing reforms that followed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1911</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> succeeds, a result to which the failure of the late Qing reforms contributed</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang.jpg" alt="Li Hongzhang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Li_Hung_Chang_in_1896(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Russell &amp; Sons, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After its defeat in the First (1839–1842) and Second (1856–1860) <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opium-wars">Opium Wars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> began an effort to modernise that became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-strengthening-movement">Self-Strengthening Movement</a>. Military modernisation was a high priority from the start, and the movement concentrated more on supplying the armed forces with modern weapons than on reforming governance or society. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> and others wanted a modernised military that joined western innovations to China, and they and a majority believed that through western influence China could strengthen its economy and protect itself on a global stage. The British military had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flintlock">flintlocks</a>, caplocks and breech-loading rifled artillery, while the Qing had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlock</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a> with far fewer rounds and lower accuracy.</p><p>China also wished to improve its own weapon manufacturing by building more domestic factories. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangnan-arsenal">Jiangnan Arsenal</a>, established in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> in 1865, was the largest factory in China and produced ships, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/breech-loading-rifle">breech-loading rifles</a> and artillery shells. Even so, it was considered underdeveloped beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>'s, and it was deprived of imperial funding. The military lacked the organisation, training and institutions needed to sustain coming wars.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-sino-japanese-war">First Sino-Japanese War</a> (1894–1895), in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing China</a> was defeated by Meiji <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, a state that had undergone more comprehensive reforms in the same period, exposed these limits. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-shimonoseki">Treaty of Shimonoseki</a> forced China to recognise Korean independence, cede <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaodong">Liaodong</a> Peninsula and pay a large indemnity. Chinese elites viewed the defeat as clear evidence that the Self-Strengthening Movement had failed and as a sign that institutional and constitutional change was required.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aims">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aims</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kang-youwei.jpg" alt="Kang Youwei" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kang_Yu-wei_cph.3a45114.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elmer Chickering, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Reformist thinkers such as Tan Sitong, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao responded to what they saw as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s deep crisis, which included political division, insurrection, opium addiction and growing foreign conflicts. They worked through memorials to the throne, reform and study societies and widely circulated political essays. Influenced by the success of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>'s Meiji constitutional reforms, they argued that China needed change to its governing institutions, its educational systems and its social order, not only better armies and arsenals.</p><p>The proposals covered several fields. In education they included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>, where the sciences, liberal arts and Chinese classics were to be studied together, a modern system teaching mathematics and science rather than mainly Confucian texts, a gradual replacement of the civil service examination curriculum and study abroad for members of the imperial family. In government they included a transition to a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> on the Meiji model, with deliberative institutions and limits on autocratic rule, and the abolition of the traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a>, which reformers saw as an obstacle to a modern bureaucracy and to social mobility.</p><p>In the economy they proposed ending sinecures, encouraging joint stock enterprises and private investment, founding trade schools and creating a bureau for railways and mines, with agricultural schools in every province. In the military they proposed Western-style training, drilling and staff systems, a modern military academy and a modern naval academy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="events">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Course of Events</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor.jpg" alt="Guangxu Emperor" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%E5%85%89%E7%BB%AA%E5%B8%9D.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Elements of the Qing government were alarmed enough to permit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao to put reforms to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>, who agreed and committed himself to the programme. From 11 June to 21 September 1898 the throne issued a rapid series of edicts on education, administration, the economy and the military. Some measures were carried out, such as the founding of the Imperial University of Peking, but most met resistance.</p><p>Opposition among the conservative ruling elite was intense. They condemned the reforms as too radical and proposed more moderate, gradual alternatives. Senior <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> princes, Grand Councilors and provincial governors resisted, many of them viewing the revision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-examination">examination system</a> and the abolition of sinecures as direct threats to the existing order. Conservatives feared that foreign advisers and Japanese models would place the Qing state under foreign influence, and Prince Duan urged the expulsion of Timothy Richard and the former prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> from the court. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> supported the principles of the reform, but feared that sudden implementation without bureaucratic support would be disruptive and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and other foreign powers would exploit any weakness.</p><p>As tensions between the emperor and Cixi grew, some reformers plotted to remove her by force. According to later accounts, Tan Sitong asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> to kill Ronglu, take control of the garrison at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tianjin">Tientsin</a> and march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> to arrest Cixi. Yuan had earlier promised to support Ronglu and told him of the plot, which let Cixi and her faction prepare a coup d'état. With the support of the conservatives and the armed forces commanded by Yuan and Ronglu, Cixi took over the government in September 1898.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="people">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">People</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi.jpg" alt="Itō Hirobumi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IT%C5%8C_Hirobumi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> initiated the reform. According to Sterling Seagrave, he bypassed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a> and appointed four reformers to advise him after a series of interviews, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> was rejected. He also held secret talks with the former Japanese prime minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a>, hoping to use his experience of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration</a>. Guangxu was put under house arrest on Yingtai Island until his death in 1908.</p><p>Kang Youwei and his student Liang Qichao were the two principal leaders, scholars who proposed the reforms to the emperor. Tan Sitong, a reformist thinker, refused to flee after the coup and was executed. The "Six Gentlemen of Wuxu" were Tan Sitong, Kang Guangren (Kang Youwei's brother), Lin Xu, Yang Shenxiu, Yang Rui and Liu Guangdi. Students of Kang and Liang formed the Kang Party, which was one of the groups that most alarmed court conservatives.</p><p>On the other side stood <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a>, with allies among the conservative elite, and the armed forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> and Ronglu. The generals Dong Fuxiang, Ma Anliang and Ma Haiyan were called to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and helped end the movement, along with Ma Fulu and Ma Fuxiang. Dong Fuxiang and the Muslim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a> Army stationed in Beijing later took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boxer-rebellion">Boxer Rebellion</a> and became known as the Kansu Braves. The British missionary Timothy Richard, whom Kang invited to Beijing, also figures in the events of September 1898.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="outcomes">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Outcomes</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The coup reversed the reforms. The old writing style was put back into mandate, the offices and agencies that had been removed were reinstituted, and the establishment of certain newspapers, civil societies and schools was suspended. The chief advocates, the Six Gentlemen of Wuxu, were ordered to be executed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, where they founded the Baohuang Hui (Protect the Emperor Society) and worked, unsuccessfully, for a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>.</p><p>An alternative view holds that Kang and Liang had a different aim in leaving for Japan: not only to justify fleeing China but also to solicit intervention by foreign powers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> or Japan to rescue the emperor.</p><p>The late Qing reforms after 1901 included the abolition of the imperial examination in 1905, educational and military modernisation patterned on Japan, and experiments in constitutional and parliamentary government. Their ultimate failure gave impetus to revolutionary forces: changes within the establishment came to be seen as largely hopeless, and the overthrow of the Qing increasingly appeared the only way to save China. Such sentiments directly contributed to the success of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinhai-revolution">Xinhai Revolution</a> in 1911.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi.jpg" alt="Empress Dowager Cixi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Ci-Xi_Imperial_Dowager_Empress_(9.2).PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Yu Shuinling, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Although brief, the movement introduced new political concepts of nationhood and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and it inspired many of the "New Policies" reforms launched after 1901. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Cixi</a> herself backed the late Qing reforms after the invasions of the Eight-Nation Alliance. The Imperial University of Peking, founded in the movement, is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a>.</p><p>Historians differ over the failure. The mainstream view is that the reformers were heroic but unorganised, trying to reform and save a backward state but hampered by the ruling conservative faction led by Cixi, who feared the results would impede their own power. Some historians of the late 20th century take a view more favourable to the conservatives and less to the reformers: that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and his allies were hopeless dreamers unaware of the political realities, and that the conservative elites were not opposed to change. Sterling Seagrave, in The Dragon Lady, argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> nobles of the xenophobic iron hats faction dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing">Grand Council</a>, and that Kang had far less influence than his later boasting suggested. It has also been suggested, controversially, that Kang's perceived arrogance harmed the cause and that false rumours reached the Grand Council.</p><p>Professor Lei Chia-sheng argues that Timothy Richard's plan of 18 September, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> would join a federation of ten nations, convinced Cixi to end the reforms with the coup. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-tolstoy">Leo Tolstoy</a> corresponded with Gu Hongming on the reform and agreed that it was ill-advised.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Hundred Days' Reform was a movement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> from 11 June to 21 September 1898, led by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kang-youwei">Kang Youwei</a> and Liang Qichao, which sought to modernise <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s institutions after defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> ended it with a coup, and the leading reformers were ordered executed.</em></p>

